> Ekemon and his team are at a archane library, and Ekemon wants to use it to find the location of multiple belts of giant strength. I'd like to come up with a fun mechanic that makes this more than just a series of skill checks. Two ideas I had were:
>  - I could make Austin give a series of queries, each of which reveals text, from which he can get special keywords that help him get the next right query, until he has-- say-- the keyword describing the region, locale, entrance, etc. But I want it to be possible to end up going down wrong paths and reaching dead ends, but also getting lesser prizes, like real literature searches.
>  - I was also thinking that the library could be build like a maze/dungeon, where he searches in room after room, trying to get closer to where he wants to go. But it's not always clear if he's going the right direction, and there are many branches, and so similarly he can end up in deadends or with lesser prizes.
> 
> What do you think is actually implementable for us?
**assistant**:
> I’d use a **hybrid “living catalog” clue-web**, rather than simulate either real research or a full dungeon maze. It gives Austin meaningful choices without becoming confusing or requiring you to improvise a whole library.
> 
> ### The Arcane Library Hunt
> 
> Make **6–8 illustrated “research locations”** (cards or simple boxes on paper):  
> 
> - **Grand Catalog** — choose a starting subject  
> - **Giants’ Histories**  
> - **Relic Vault Index**  
> - **Explorer Journals**  
> - **Forbidden/Locked Stacks**  
> - **Map Room**  
> - **Curator’s Office**  
> - **Dead-end shelves**  
> 
> Each location offers 2–3 **search approaches**—e.g., “Look for fire giants,” “Look for old treasure maps,” or “Ask the ghost librarian.” A choice reveals a clue token: a word, symbol, map fragment, or name.
> 
> To reach a real belt location, Ekemon needs a small “address” of clues:
> 
> > **Giant kind + region + landmark/entrance**  
> > e.g. **Frost Giant + Shiverpeak + Behind the Singing Icefall**
> 
> Once he has all three, the Map Room can identify that belt’s site.
> 
> ### Make wrong paths productive
> 
> Every lead should give *something*, even if it doesn’t complete a belt address:
> 
> - a minor magic item or scroll
> - a friendly scholar/contact
> - lore that helps against giants
> - a rumor about a different Titan component
> - a shortcut, secret door, or library favor
> - a “danger clue” that tells him a site is too hard *for now*
> 
> A dead end can be phrased as: “This belt was melted in a volcano long ago—but the book contains a **Fire Giant recipe** for a strength potion.” That feels like research, not failure.
> 
> ### Add one simple pressure mechanic
> 
> Use a **Library Trouble track**, 0–3. Each search takes time or risks attention; on a failed check, advance Trouble instead of blocking the clue. At 3, introduce a fun complication: a rival researcher, escaped book-creatures, or a strict librarian demanding a favor.
> 
> ### Why this is best for your table
> 
> A literal maze risks arbitrary wandering, while chained text queries can become a guessing game. The clue-web is visible, choice-driven, and kid-friendly: Austin gets to say, “We need the **entrance clue**—let’s go to Explorer Journals!” Yet it still permits surprising detours and real-feeling discoveries.
